speechtext:
Yes, and I got out. The economic problem is not a simple one. I would like, if I may, to say to hon. members, and particularly to the hon. member for Outremont (Mr. Vien), who was in the house so many years ago, that I am not offering captious criticism. I am not desiring to criticize this government at all. I am not talking about the government at all. I am simply trying to discuss the problem for the purpose of laying at least a foundation from which to attack it. I am not saying that the solutions are here or there, but I do say that we in this parliament, if we will recognize the underlying and fundamental principles, upon that foundation can build and make a great contribution. The trouble is that we will not recognize these principles. I am not
prepared to accept the doctrines of my social credit friends, but the hon. gentleman who leads that party in the house (Mr. Black-more) last night made a speech that is challenging to hon. members. Not his solutions;
I am not talking about them.
